Sustainability Greenwashing Detection on Social Media
Principal Investigator DR. Wenli HUANG

Overview
This project investigates the role of social media platforms in the phenomenon of greenwashing — the practice of making misleading or unsubstantiated claims about the environmental benefits of a product, service, or company.

Core Research Question
Does social media, as a platform, enhance (amplify and enable) or discourage (expose and reduce) greenwashing practices?

The study combines computational detection of greenwashing signals with sentiment analysis of both original posts and user comments to assess how platform dynamics shape corporate sustainability communication and public response.

Research Objectives
Detect linguistic and rhetorical markers of greenwashing in sustainability-related social media content.
Analyse the sentiment of primary posts that contain sustainability or environmental claims.
Examine the sentiment of public comments/replies to those posts to understand audience reception, scepticism, or endorsement.
Evaluate whether social media mechanisms (virality, user scrutiny, algorithmic amplification) tend to increase the visibility and acceptance of greenwashing or, conversely, facilitate its detection and criticism.
