Methodology Snapshot
1. Data Collection
Public posts from major social media platforms containing sustainability-related language.
Associated comment threads for reciprocal sentiment analysis.
2. Greenwashing Signal Detection
Keyword and phrase matching against curated greenwashing lexicons.
Contextual feature extraction (hedging language, absence of quantifiable metrics, appeal to emotion over evidence).
Classification of posts into categories: credible sustainability communication vs. potential greenwashing.
3. Sentiment Analysis
Post Sentiment: Overall polarity and emotional tone of the original sustainability claim (positive framing, urgency, pride, etc.).
Comment Sentiment: Aggregate and distributional sentiment of user replies, with particular attention to:
Scepticism / accusation of greenwashing
Support / endorsement
Neutral information-seeking
Irony or sarcasm
4. Platform Effect Analysis
Correlation between detected greenwashing signals and engagement metrics.
Comparison of comment sentiment polarity when greenwashing markers are present versus absent.
Temporal and network patterns that indicate amplification or pushback.
Expected Contributions
Empirical evidence on whether social media primarily functions as an enabler (broad reach, low verification costs, positive framing incentives) or a deterrent (collective scrutiny, rapid call-outs, reputational risk) of greenwashing.
A reusable detection pipeline combining keyword-based signals and dual-level (post + comment) sentiment analysis.
Insights for regulators, platforms, brands, and civil-society actors on the dynamics of environmental claim credibility online.
